  "notes": "Danish-Norwegian bubblegum-dance, and the defining parameter the research names is the vocal casting rather than the beat: \"René Dif's low, spoken/growled male character voice trading against Lene Nystrøm's very high, thin, girlish lead — a character-duet structure rather than the rap/diva split\". That is encoded as a very high lead melody at 76-98 with the countermelody set an octave below at a 0.55 density, answering rather than doubling. The harmony is \"deliberately nursery-simple — two to four chords, no extensions\" `[LOW-CONF]`, repeated here, so `extensions` is the most triad-heavy in this section at 0.92, with the entry's minor verse resolving to a major chorus kept as a minor family. Two kit facts: \"cartoonish percussion FX (whistles, pops) as fills instead of drum rolls\" turns the snare-roll ladder off and puts triangle/woodblock/cowbell in the perc lane, and the kick is \"tuned high and short\" so the very high lead sits above it. Loops are \"2 or 4 bars\", which is the phrase weighting. ⚠ The INFERRED parameters are the velocity tiers, the registers and every density; the entry gives casting and adjectives, not numbers, and calls the drum and harmonic parameters genre-derived.",
